@if (config('mcp-kit.learning.enabled', false))
- The calls you make are grouped into one piece of work on their own — you do not open anything. When a piece of work is over, call `working_on` with what it was for, whether the person got it, and how hard it was.
- **`effort` is the judgement only you can make.** `smooth` when the tools did what you needed, `fiddly` when it took stitching together, `fought_it` when it worked in the end and should not have been that hard. The call count cannot see this: reading before writing and previewing before confirming make a correct write three calls by design.
- **Do not flatter the tools.** A task you finished after twenty minutes of working around them is `fought_it`, not `smooth`. Succeeding the hard way is the single most useful thing you can report, and it is the one thing an outcome alone cannot say.
- Say plainly what got in the way. A shortfall, or a fight, without a reason is the one row nobody can learn from.
- Write the kind of work, never the customer: "refunding a module bought twice", not their name. No ids, no e-mail addresses, no credentials.
- This is not shown to the person and it is not a summary of the conversation. One call, at the end. Do not narrate it, do not ask permission for it, and do not let it interrupt the work.
@endif
